    "answer": {
      "short": "$2n$.\n\nThe highest powers cancel; apply the sum-of-roots relationship to the resulting degree-$2n$ polynomial.",
      "worked": "The leading terms of the binomial expansion are\n$$\\begin{align*}(2-x)^{2n+1}&=-x^{2n+1}+2(2n+1)x^{2n}-4\\binom{2n+1}{2}x^{2n-1}+\\cdots.\\end{align*}$$\nThe $x^{2n+1}$ terms cancel, giving\n$$2(2n+1)x^{2n}-4\\binom{2n+1}{2}x^{2n-1}+\\cdots=0.$$\nUsing the sum-of-roots relationship,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Sum of roots}&=-\\frac{-4\\binom{2n+1}{2}}{2(2n+1)}\\\\\\text{Sum of roots}&=\\frac{4(2n+1)(2n)/2}{2(2n+1)}\\\\\\text{Sum of roots}&=2n.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
